WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
Question 0010: valid parentheses,
a student-level question.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:09.833
Two parts: first a simple solution,
then one that reads the string once.

3
00:00:10.633 --> 00:00:15.867
A string holds brackets of three kinds:
round, square and curly.

4
00:00:15.867 --> 00:00:21.333
It is valid if every opener is closed by the same kind,
and the last one opened is closed first.

5
00:00:22.133 --> 00:00:25.533
Part A: a simple solution,
even if it is not efficient.

6
00:00:26.233 --> 00:00:29.233
The page's string: {[()]}.

7
00:00:29.233 --> 00:00:34.333
The idea: a valid, non-empty string
always has an adjacent pair, like () or [].

8
00:00:34.333 --> 00:00:39.133
Delete it. What is left is valid
if and only if the original string is valid.

9
00:00:39.133 --> 00:00:43.733
Now [] is adjacent, and after it {}.
Delete them too.

10
00:00:43.733 --> 00:00:47.233
Nothing is left: the string is valid.

11
00:00:48.833 --> 00:00:53.867
A deletion can create a new adjacent pair:
in (()) we delete the inner pair,

12
00:00:53.867 --> 00:00:59.167
and only then the outer pair becomes adjacent.
So we repeat the deletion, pass after pass.

13
00:00:59.967 --> 00:01:04.767
And the string ([)] has no adjacent pair:
nothing to delete, and it is not empty.

14
00:01:04.767 --> 00:01:09.733
So it is not valid: the round bracket closes
before the square one, opened after it.

15
00:01:10.533 --> 00:01:13.533
Now let's write it in Python.

16
00:01:15.833 --> 00:01:20.333
isValid_brute takes the string s,
and returns True if it is valid.

17
00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:27.900
Line 2: the loop runs while the string has
an adjacent pair, of any of the three kinds.

18
00:01:32.033 --> 00:01:36.533
Line 3: one pass deletes every (),
then every [], and then every {}.

19
00:01:37.867 --> 00:01:42.367
Line 4: when no adjacent pair is left,
the string is valid only if nothing is left.

20
00:01:43.067 --> 00:01:45.867
Let's run the code on (()).

21
00:01:45.867 --> 00:01:48.967
It has an adjacent pair, so we enter the loop.

22
00:01:48.967 --> 00:01:53.267
Line 3: the first pass deletes
the inner pair, and () is left.

23
00:01:53.267 --> 00:01:57.067
The deletion created a new adjacent pair,
so the loop goes on.

24
00:01:57.067 --> 00:02:01.367
The second pass deletes it,
and an empty string is left.

25
00:02:01.367 --> 00:02:04.367
No adjacent pair is left,
and the loop ends.

26
00:02:04.367 --> 00:02:08.367
Line 4: the string is empty,
so True is returned.

27
00:02:09.667 --> 00:02:13.967
([)] has no adjacent pair,
so the loop does not even start.

28
00:02:13.967 --> 00:02:17.967
Line 4: four characters are left,
so False is returned.

29
00:02:17.967 --> 00:02:23.467
Note the loop's condition: there is an adjacent pair.
With "the string is not empty" it would never end.

30
00:02:24.267 --> 00:02:28.567
What does it cost? Take a nested string,
(((()))), of length 8.

31
00:02:28.567 --> 00:02:34.467
Each pass deletes only the inner pair:
after two passes, (()) is left.

32
00:02:34.467 --> 00:02:39.867
Two more passes, and only then is the string empty:
four passes in all.

33
00:02:39.867 --> 00:02:45.367
Each pass reads the string three times,
once per replace: 44 characters, against 8.

34
00:02:46.167 --> 00:02:51.333
The page's box shows the passes too,
and the characters read in each one.

35
00:02:51.333 --> 00:02:55.333
A nested string of length n takes n/2 passes,
each costs O(n): O(n²) in all.

36
00:02:56.133 --> 00:03:01.633
The cost: O(n²) time, and O(n) memory
for the new strings. We can do better.

37
00:03:02.433 --> 00:03:06.233
Part B: a solution that reads the string
only once.

38
00:03:06.933 --> 00:03:10.533
The idea: the bracket opened last
is closed first.

39
00:03:10.533 --> 00:03:15.566
When a closer comes, only the last opener
not closed yet can match it.

40
00:03:15.566 --> 00:03:19.100
That is exactly a stack:
last in, first out.

41
00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:24.700
This is the page's box. The stack is on the left,
and the stack's depth in the chart.

42
00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:27.833
{ is an opener, so it is pushed onto the stack.

43
00:03:27.833 --> 00:03:30.966
[ is an opener too, pushed on top of it.

44
00:03:30.966 --> 00:03:34.100
( is pushed as well, and it is now
at the top of the stack.

45
00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:38.700
) is a closer. The top is ( of the same kind,
so we pop it.

46
00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:41.833
] closes the [ at the top of the stack.

47
00:03:41.833 --> 00:03:45.866
} closes the {. The stack is empty:
the string is valid.

48
00:03:46.666 --> 00:03:49.566
Now ([)], which has no adjacent pair.

49
00:03:49.566 --> 00:03:53.833
( and then [ are pushed onto the stack.

50
00:03:53.833 --> 00:03:58.733
) closes a round bracket, but the top is [:
not valid, and we stop at once.

51
00:03:59.533 --> 00:04:04.933
And in ((( every character is an opener,
and all are pushed onto the stack.

52
00:04:04.933 --> 00:04:08.933
The string has ended and the stack is not empty:
three openers were never closed.

53
00:04:08.933 --> 00:04:12.933
These are the three failures: a wrong kind,
a closer with no opener, and openers left over.

54
00:04:13.733 --> 00:04:16.733
Now let's write it in Python.

55
00:04:18.566 --> 00:04:22.666
isValid takes a string,
and returns True if it is valid.

56
00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:29.700
Lines 2–3: an odd length is never valid,
because brackets come in pairs.

57
00:04:32.433 --> 00:04:36.233
Line 5: a dictionary from each closer
to the opener of its kind.

58
00:04:37.233 --> 00:04:40.400
Line 6: the stack, an empty list at first.

59
00:04:41.633 --> 00:04:45.166
Line 8: we go over the characters,
one after the other.

60
00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:55.900
Lines 9–11: a closer needs its opener
at the top of the stack. If not, return False.

61
00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:59.566
Line 12: if it is there,
we pop it off the stack.

62
00:05:01.666 --> 00:05:04.900
Lines 13–14: an opener
is pushed onto the stack.

63
00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:10.800
Line 16: at the end, valid only if the stack is empty,
that is, every opener was closed.

64
00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:14.700
Let's run it on {[()]},
beside the page's box.

65
00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:19.100
The length is 6, even. The dictionary is ready,
and the stack is empty.

66
00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:24.633
{ is an opener: line 14 pushes it.

67
00:05:24.633 --> 00:05:30.166
[ is pushed too.

68
00:05:30.166 --> 00:05:35.700
( is pushed too. Now the stack
holds three openers.

69
00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:41.233
) is a closer, and the top is (:
line 12 pops it.

70
00:05:41.233 --> 00:05:46.766
] closes the [.

71
00:05:46.766 --> 00:05:52.300
} closes the {.

72
00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:56.400
The loop has ended and the stack is empty:
line 16 returns True.

73
00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:02.000
Now ([)]: the length is 4, even.

74
00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:07.533
( is pushed.

75
00:06:07.533 --> 00:06:13.066
[ is pushed on top of it.

76
00:06:13.066 --> 00:06:18.600
) is a closer, and the top is [:
line 11 returns False.

77
00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:25.100
And on ((( the code stops at line 3 already:
the length is 3, odd, before reading a character.

78
00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:29.100
The box reads to the end and shows the openers
left over. The code knows it earlier, from the length.

79
00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:35.466
And openers left over at an even length? Say (([]:
the length is 4, so the loop runs.

80
00:06:35.466 --> 00:06:41.000
The three openers are pushed onto the stack.

81
00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:46.533
] closes the [, and line 12 pops it.

82
00:06:46.533 --> 00:06:51.066
The loop has ended, and (( is left on the stack:
line 16 returns False.

83
00:06:51.866 --> 00:06:57.366
The cost: each character is read once, O(n) time,
and O(n) memory for the stack at worst.

84
00:06:58.166 --> 00:07:03.066
A trap: counting openers and closers is not enough.
)( has one of each.

85
00:07:03.066 --> 00:07:07.800
But the closer comes while the stack is empty,
and lines 10–11 return False.

86
00:07:08.593 --> 00:07:13.627
In short: deleting adjacent pairs, O(n²),
or a stack in one pass, O(n).

87
00:07:13.627 --> 00:07:17.727
Try strings of your own in the box
on the page of question 0010 on the site.
